# ALLOWLIST. Exclude everything, then re-admit exactly the six entries the spec
# names, plus the two license files pyproject.toml's license-files requires.
#
# This is an allowlist because a denylist loses. A denylist would have to name
# .env -- and then .env.local, .env.production, the *.pem somebody drops in
# while debugging TLS, the whole ~/.aws a contributor copies into the checkout.
# An allowlist refuses all of them by default and refuses the next one too.
#
# BuildKit is required for this file to be read at all: the legacy builder
# looks only for .dockerignore at the context root, and this repo has none. So
# a legacy build would send the entire checkout -- .env included -- to the
# daemon. hosted/image/build.sh sets DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 rather than trusting
# that the CLI still defaults to it.
*
.*

!waku/
!skills/
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
!README.md
!LICENSE
!LICENSE-BRAND

# Re-excluded inside the admitted trees: build noise, and the maintainers' own
# runtime state, neither of which belongs in a tenant's image. Last match wins,
# so these beat the !waku/ above.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/

# Secrets, re-excluded INSIDE the admitted trees. The lines above this block
# admit whole directories, and an allowlist that only names top-level entries
# cannot see a waku/.env or a skills/deploy.pem that somebody wrote while
# debugging -- it rides in with the tree that admits it. These four patterns
# are the only part of this file that is not about build noise, and they must
# stay BELOW the `!` lines: Docker is last-match-wins, so the same four lines
# above the admissions would be undone by them.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
